1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Imagine your computer
starts behaving strangely.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:08,660
A program freezes. Something crashes.
What do you do? You open the logs.

3
00:00:09,401 --> 00:00:11,481
You check what happened before the crash.

4
00:00:12,281 --> 00:00:13,941
You look for the error message.

5
00:00:14,541 --> 00:00:17,601
And if you're lucky,
you find the exact line responsible.

6
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,280
Now imagine your own body
doing the same thing.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:26,200
You wake up with a headache.
You feel exhausted. Your stomach hurts.

8
00:00:26,945 --> 00:00:30,425
Something is clearly wrong,
but the only message you get is this.

9
00:00:30,825 --> 00:00:33,085
Error. Something went wrong.

10
00:00:33,505 --> 00:00:35,025
Root cause: unknown.

11
00:00:35,656 --> 00:00:38,976
And the really strange part?
It's your body.

12
00:00:39,516 --> 00:00:41,436
Surely, you're the system administrator.

13
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:44,216
Your brain adjusts your heartbeat,

14
00:00:44,296 --> 00:00:47,376
regulates your temperature,
and talks to your immune system.

15
00:00:48,003 --> 00:00:51,743
Yet you can't open a terminal and ask
what happened to me last night?

16
00:00:52,423 --> 00:00:54,503
So why can't you read your own logs?

17
00:00:56,963 --> 00:00:59,503
In 1995, the British Medical Journal

18
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:02,603
described a builder
who had jumped down onto a nail.

19
00:01:03,143 --> 00:01:05,303
Fifteen centimetres long.

20
00:01:05,923 --> 00:01:07,703
It went straight through his boot.

21
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:10,110
The smallest movement of that nail hurt

22
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:12,550
so much that doctors gave him fentanyl

23
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,710
and a sedative before pulling it out.

24
00:01:16,395 --> 00:01:17,935
Then they took off the boot.

25
00:01:18,335 --> 00:01:20,435
The nail had passed between his toes.

26
00:01:21,035 --> 00:01:23,195
His foot was completely unharmed.

27
00:01:24,605 --> 00:01:29,205
Ten years later, in Colorado, a
construction worker's nail gun backfired.

28
00:01:29,445 --> 00:01:32,245
He felt a heavy blow,
and then a toothache.

29
00:01:32,895 --> 00:01:35,995
For six days, he took painkillers
and iced his face.

30
00:01:36,755 --> 00:01:40,055
Then he went to a dentist,
and an X-ray showed why.

31
00:01:40,673 --> 00:01:45,193
A ten-centimetre nail, through the roof
of his mouth and into his brain.

32
00:01:45,693 --> 00:01:49,353
Surgeons took it out,
and he was expected to recover fully.

33
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:54,826
One man had no injury at all,
and pain that needed a strong opioid.

34
00:01:55,646 --> 00:01:58,466
The other had a nail in his brain,
and a toothache.

35
00:01:59,069 --> 00:02:01,889
So your body's error messages
aren't just vague.

36
00:02:02,569 --> 00:02:04,569
They can be wrong in both directions.

37
00:02:05,289 --> 00:02:07,729
But that isn't because
nothing is being recorded.

38
00:02:10,379 --> 00:02:15,059
One nerve, the vagus, connects your brain
to your heart, your lungs and your gut.

39
00:02:15,839 --> 00:02:17,139
And by the usual estimate,

40
00:02:17,219 --> 00:02:20,679
around eighty percent of its fibres
don't carry orders down.

41
00:02:21,239 --> 00:02:22,899
They carry reports up.

42
00:02:23,493 --> 00:02:25,633
Sensing your own insides even has a name.

43
00:02:25,863 --> 00:02:27,513
Interoception.

44
00:02:28,313 --> 00:02:30,693
Some of it reaches you. You feel hungry.

45
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:34,813
You notice your heart racing.
Most of it never does.

46
00:02:35,458 --> 00:02:37,338
You can test this right now.

47
00:02:38,018 --> 00:02:41,678
Without touching your wrist or your neck,
try to count your heartbeats.

48
00:02:47,598 --> 00:02:51,138
In the lab version of this test,
most people count too few.

49
00:02:51,838 --> 00:02:54,678
And that's an organ
thumping away inside your chest.

50
00:02:55,776 --> 00:02:57,736
Part of the reason may be capacity.

51
00:02:58,616 --> 00:03:00,236
Every day, your body replaces

52
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:02,976
about three hundred
and thirty billion cells,

53
00:03:03,136 --> 00:03:04,796
most of them blood cells.

54
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:10,070
And one recent estimate puts human thought
and action at about ten bits per second.

55
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:14,350
By the same estimate,
your senses take in around a billion.

56
00:03:14,721 --> 00:03:17,801
So even with every signal on a screen,
you couldn't keep up.

57
00:03:18,321 --> 00:03:20,061
The terminal is too narrow.

58
00:03:20,601 --> 00:03:24,161
But capacity alone doesn't explain
which few lines get through.

59
00:03:26,661 --> 00:03:30,601
People who keep huge computer systems
running have a rule about this.

60
00:03:31,261 --> 00:03:35,101
It's in Google's book
on site reliability, in five words.

61
00:03:35,761 --> 00:03:37,701
Every page should be actionable.

62
00:03:38,239 --> 00:03:41,079
A page is an alert
that wakes up an engineer.

63
00:03:41,819 --> 00:03:45,419
If nobody can do anything about it,
it shouldn't wake anybody up.

64
00:03:46,395 --> 00:03:48,755
Now look at what your body pages you for.

65
00:03:49,095 --> 00:03:54,895
Hunger. Eat. Thirst. Drink.
A full bladder. Find a bathroom.

66
00:03:55,235 --> 00:03:57,295
A hand on a hot stove. Move.

67
00:03:57,936 --> 00:04:03,016
In 1979, the pain researcher Patrick Wall
argued that pain has more in common

68
00:04:03,246 --> 00:04:06,076
with hunger and thirst
than with seeing or hearing.

69
00:04:07,076 --> 00:04:09,376
A demand for action, not a measurement.

70
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:12,610
Now look at what stays silent.

71
00:04:12,970 --> 00:04:15,930
High blood pressure
usually has no symptoms.

72
00:04:16,850 --> 00:04:20,350
About a third of adults aged thirty
to seventy-nine have it,

73
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:22,310
and nearly half of them don't know.

74
00:04:22,875 --> 00:04:26,095
In one large American study,
more than forty-five percent

75
00:04:26,175 --> 00:04:30,695
of first heart attacks
were only discovered later, on an ECG.

76
00:04:31,335 --> 00:04:36,015
Whatever those people felt at the time,
nobody took it for a heart attack.

77
00:04:37,048 --> 00:04:38,648
For nearly all of human history,

78
00:04:38,728 --> 00:04:41,388
nobody could do anything
about their blood pressure.

79
00:04:42,128 --> 00:04:46,208
No action, so perhaps, no alert.
That's a hypothesis.

80
00:04:47,148 --> 00:04:49,808
Nobody can rerun evolution to check it.

81
00:04:50,777 --> 00:04:53,537
There's a second rule,
and the physician Randolph Nesse

82
00:04:53,617 --> 00:04:55,657
named it after a smoke detector.

83
00:04:56,657 --> 00:04:59,137
A false alarm costs you some burnt toast.

84
00:04:59,857 --> 00:05:01,657
A missed one costs you the house.

85
00:05:02,311 --> 00:05:07,371
So defences like pain, fever and vomiting
may be tuned to go off far too often.

86
00:05:07,971 --> 00:05:11,371
Seen that way, the builder's body
wasn't malfunctioning.

87
00:05:11,611 --> 00:05:13,911
A nail through your boot
is exactly when you want

88
00:05:14,141 --> 00:05:16,371
the alarm first and the facts later.

89
00:05:19,011 --> 00:05:21,971
And sometimes,
the alarm watches the wrong number.

90
00:05:22,731 --> 00:05:25,551
When you hold your breath,
that desperate urge comes mainly

91
00:05:25,781 --> 00:05:30,111
from carbon dioxide building up,
not from oxygen running out.

92
00:05:30,393 --> 00:05:34,073
So if your oxygen falls
while carbon dioxide keeps leaving,

93
00:05:34,213 --> 00:05:36,073
that alarm barely goes off.

94
00:05:36,713 --> 00:05:39,853
Pilots are trained for this,
because some people feel euphoric,

95
00:05:40,083 --> 00:05:42,013
and may not know they're in trouble.

96
00:05:42,651 --> 00:05:48,411
In 2005, Helios Flight 522
climbed with its cabin pressure falling.

97
00:05:48,991 --> 00:05:53,631
A warning horn sounded, and the crew seem
to have taken it for a different warning.

98
00:05:54,248 --> 00:05:58,688
A ground engineer on the radio even asked
about the pressurisation panel.

99
00:05:59,408 --> 00:06:02,688
The captain replied by asking
where the cooling breakers were.

100
00:06:03,248 --> 00:06:05,028
Then the crew stopped answering.

101
00:06:05,728 --> 00:06:07,988
A hundred and twenty-one people died.

102
00:06:10,238 --> 00:06:12,878
Now imagine that alarm hardly working.

103
00:06:13,598 --> 00:06:17,978
Around a thousand people worldwide have
a condition once called Ondine's curse.

104
00:06:18,699 --> 00:06:23,459
Their automatic breathing barely responds
to changes in oxygen and carbon dioxide.

105
00:06:24,379 --> 00:06:27,379
Some need a ventilator
every time they fall asleep.

106
00:06:28,099 --> 00:06:30,059
Some need one all the time.

107
00:06:32,599 --> 00:06:36,219
And even when an alert does fire,
it can carry the wrong address.

108
00:06:37,179 --> 00:06:41,099
A heart attack can show up as pain
in your arm, your back, or your jaw.

109
00:06:41,519 --> 00:06:44,899
Something cold on the roof of your mouth
hurts in your forehead.

110
00:06:45,584 --> 00:06:48,624
The best-known explanation
is a shared line.

111
00:06:48,764 --> 00:06:53,804
Nerves from your organs and your skin meet
on the same neurons in your spinal cord.

112
00:06:54,324 --> 00:06:56,564
Two machines behind one address.

113
00:06:57,047 --> 00:07:00,607
The brain sees a signal on that line,
and blames the skin,

114
00:07:00,687 --> 00:07:03,307
because that's where signals
usually come from.

115
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:06,360
None of this means you should ignore it.

116
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:09,480
Pain can be bad at saying where and why.

117
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:13,860
New or unexplained chest pain
is still a reason to get help, now.

118
00:07:14,789 --> 00:07:16,929
Then there's the oddest gap of all.

119
00:07:17,449 --> 00:07:19,969
Brain tissue itself has no pain receptors.

120
00:07:20,909 --> 00:07:25,549
Surgeons can operate on awake patients
once the scalp and the membranes are numb.

121
00:07:26,153 --> 00:07:28,993
That headache you woke up with
isn't your brain hurting.

122
00:07:29,653 --> 00:07:32,173
It comes mostly
from the tissues around it.

123
00:07:32,793 --> 00:07:36,293
The organ reading the alerts
can't raise that one about itself.

124
00:07:38,943 --> 00:07:42,383
So is there any log at all?
There is. There are several...

125
00:07:42,463 --> 00:07:44,363
You've just never been shown them.

126
00:07:45,065 --> 00:07:48,505
Your blood keeps a partial record
of the viruses you've met.

127
00:07:49,185 --> 00:07:52,905
In 2015, researchers built a test
that checks a drop of blood

128
00:07:53,135 --> 00:07:56,665
for antibodies against
two hundred and six species of virus.

129
00:07:57,545 --> 00:07:59,845
The average person showed about ten.

130
00:08:00,419 --> 00:08:02,699
Your hair grows about
a centimetre a month,

131
00:08:02,779 --> 00:08:06,559
and it traps traces of a stress hormone,
cortisol, as it grows.

132
00:08:07,259 --> 00:08:10,379
A few centimetres
can hint at the last few months.

133
00:08:11,055 --> 00:08:13,195
Your DNA keeps a tally too.

134
00:08:13,935 --> 00:08:15,475
Smoking a pack a day for a year

135
00:08:15,705 --> 00:08:17,775
leaves an estimated
hundred and fifty extra

136
00:08:18,005 --> 00:08:20,175
mutations in each lung cell.

137
00:08:20,753 --> 00:08:22,873
And one log you can check yourself.

138
00:08:23,673 --> 00:08:26,253
A serious illness
can briefly interrupt your nail growth,

139
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:28,433
and leave a groove across the nail.

140
00:08:29,113 --> 00:08:31,553
It takes about six months to grow out.

141
00:08:32,539 --> 00:08:33,979
So the logs exist.

142
00:08:34,499 --> 00:08:36,219
They just were never written for you.

143
00:08:36,699 --> 00:08:39,359
An antibody is there
for your immune system.

144
00:08:39,899 --> 00:08:42,259
A hormone is there
for the cells it reaches.

145
00:08:43,119 --> 00:08:46,259
The reader was never the part of you
that's watching this video.

146
00:08:49,349 --> 00:08:52,269
That's why we had to build
the debugger on the outside.

147
00:08:53,009 --> 00:08:55,029
An ECG records your heart.

148
00:08:55,529 --> 00:08:57,509
An EEG records your brain.

149
00:08:58,189 --> 00:09:00,489
Blood tests, scanners, smartwatches.

150
00:09:00,927 --> 00:09:02,927
And each one sees only a part.

151
00:09:03,527 --> 00:09:06,787
A number can be perfectly accurate
and still be misleading.

152
00:09:07,607 --> 00:09:11,267
A hundred beats a minute
is perfectly normal after climbing stairs.

153
00:09:11,927 --> 00:09:14,487
Sitting quietly,
it might mean something else.

154
00:09:16,977 --> 00:09:19,217
So let's go back to where we started.

155
00:09:19,877 --> 00:09:21,757
Surely, you're the system administrator.

156
00:09:21,837 --> 00:09:23,037
You aren't.

157
00:09:23,583 --> 00:09:25,323
You never had root access.

158
00:09:28,181 --> 00:09:31,781
What you call you is closer
to one unprivileged process,

159
00:09:31,881 --> 00:09:34,361
with a tiny dashboard
built to make you act,

160
00:09:34,441 --> 00:09:36,401
not to tell you what's going on.

161
00:09:37,275 --> 00:09:38,955
You can measure your body.

162
00:09:39,475 --> 00:09:41,295
You can study your own brain.

163
00:09:41,935 --> 00:09:45,115
But you have to do it with instruments,
the way you'd study a stranger.

164
00:09:46,572 --> 00:09:48,832
And perhaps the strangest thing
about being human

165
00:09:49,062 --> 00:09:51,272
is that the machine
trying to understand itself

166
00:09:51,502 --> 00:09:54,492
is also the machine
doing the understanding.

167
00:09:57,422 --> 00:09:59,282
So here's a question for you.

168
00:09:59,822 --> 00:10:02,102
If you could see all of it,
every marker, every day,

169
00:10:02,332 --> 00:10:04,682
in real time, would you actually want to?

170
00:10:05,062 --> 00:10:06,452
Let me know in the comments.
